{\pard\sb200\sa120\sl360\slmult1
{\b\f0\fs36\cf2 1. Executive Summary\par}
}

{\pard\sb60\sa60\sl360\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1 Training sessions crashed or hung the GPU {\b loading the 3rd flight after 2 reverts}, every time (4 occurrences, 2026-11-23 to 2026-11-24). Measurement showed two separate problems: a {\b per-revert VRAM retention} (worst on polar flights, +0.65-0.9 GB per revert) and a {\b scene-load transient} of about {\b 2 GB above the settled level}, which takes the 12 GB card to {\b 11.6-11.8 GB} on every flight load.\par}

{\pard\sb60\sa60\sl360\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1
\bullet\tab {\b Retention is fixed.} A forced GC + Resources.UnloadUnusedAssets() after each revert (FIDO 7a2bff7) stopped the creep: 5/5 polar flights, settled VRAM 9.4-9.7 GB, no crash (before: crash on flight 3 every time).\line
\bullet\tab {\b The load peak is not overlap of old and new scenes.} An extra unload before the revert (FIDO 0432be3) freed no GPU memory; VRAM fell to 7.2 GB before the new load and still peaked at 11.76 GB. The first load from the main menu (from 1.2 GB) also peaked at 11.58 GB. The before-revert unload is now off by default (FIDO 8236fcb).\line
\bullet\tab {\b Margin is about 0.4-0.5 GB.} On Linux the NVIDIA driver does not page VRAM out to system memory the way Windows does, so going over the limit tends to crash or hang rather than slow down.\line
\bullet\tab {\b Best fits for the installed stable build (Unity 6000.4.1f1, Built-in pipeline, D3D11 via DXVK):} free desktop VRAM (0.5-0.7 GB), Texture Quality one step down, then cloud/shadow resolution or render scale. Engine-level modern methods (render-graph aliasing, temporal upscalers, GPU-driven rendering) need the Redux URP/D3D12 beta line, not this build.\par}

{\pard\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\brsp40\cf5\sb0\sa200\sl360\slmult1\par}

