{\pard\sb200\sa120\sl360\slmult1
{\b\f0\fs36\cf2 5. Modern VRAM-Reduction Methods\par}
}

{\pard\sb120\sa60\sl360\slmult1
{\b\f0\fs28\cf5 5.1 Engine Constraints That Decide Fit\par}
}

{\pard\sb60\sa60\sl360\slmult1\f0\fs22\cf1
\bullet\tab {\b Unity 6 engine, older renderer.} The engine is current (6000.4.1f1), but the installed build uses the Built-in Render Pipeline with Post-processing v2. Most of Unity 6's memory-saving rendering features (render graph with resource aliasing, STP upscaling, GPU Resident Drawer) belong to URP/HDRP only.\line
\bullet\tab {\b Assets are baked.} Texture formats, mip chains and per-texture flags (such as mipmap streaming) are set when the game is built. A mod can change runtime settings, not re-import assets.\line
\bullet\tab {\b D3D11 under DXVK.} Memory is managed by DXVK on top of Vulkan. Proton Experimental 11.0 ships DXVK newer than 2.5, whose rewritten memory manager defragments and returns unused memory (reported up to 1 GiB lower peaks in one title).\line
\bullet\tab {\b Linux NVIDIA driver.} Forum reports describe no Windows-style 'shared GPU memory' spill-over: going past VRAM tends to fail allocations, crash or hang. This matches what we saw and is why headroom matters more here.\line
\bullet\tab {\b Redux beta line differs.} Per the earlier GPU Resident Drawer report, Redux beta 26w42a moved to URP with Deferred+ and D3D12, and snapshot 0.2.9.0.108007 lists VRAM leak fixes. Methods marked 'beta only' need that move.\par}

